s-id1167066015102" class="check-understanding"><h3 data-type="title">Check Your Understanding</h3><div data-type="note" id="fs-id1167065713623" class="os-teacher ui-has-child-title"><header><h3 class="os-title" data-type="title">
  <span class="os-title-label">Teacher Support</span>
</h3></header><section>
<div class="os-note-body"><h4 data-type="title" class="os-subtitle">
  <span class="os-subtitle-label">Teacher Support</span>
</h4>
<p id="fs-id1167064774265">Use these questions to assess student achievement of the section’s learning objectives. If students are struggling with a specific objective, these questions will help identify which one, and then you can direct students to the relevant content.</p>
</div></section></div>
<div data-type="exercise" id="fs-id1167062385114" class="os-exercise"><header></header><section>
<div data-type="problem" id="fs-id1167066137414">
<span class="os-number">3</span><span class="os-divider">. </span>
<div class="os-problem-container">
<!--<para id="fs-id1167065976292">
<link class="os-embed" url="#ost/api/ex/k12phys-ch16-ex009"/>
</para>-->
<p id="fs-id1169827689043">How does the object distance, d<sub>o</sub>, compare with the focal length, f, for a concave mirror that produces an image that is real and inverted?</p>
<ol id="fs-id1169828355027" type="a"><li>d<sub>o</sub> &gt; f, where d<sub>o</sub> and f are object distance and focal length, respectively.</li>
<li>d<sub>o</sub> &lt; f, where d<sub>o</sub> and f are object distance and focal length, respectively.</li>
<li>d<sub>o</sub> = f, where do and f are object distance and focal length, respectively.</li>
<li>d<sub>o</sub> = 0, where do is the object distance.</li>
</ol></div>
</div>
</section></div>
<div data-type="injected-exercise" class="os-embed autogenerated-content" data-injected-from-nickname="" data-injected-from-version="8" data-injected-from-url="https://exercises.openstax.org/api/exercises?q=tag:k12phys-ch16-ex010" data-tags="os-practice-concepts lo:stax-phys:25-2-1 k12phys-ch16 k12phys-ch16-s01 k12phys-ch16-s01-lo01 k12phys-ch16-ex010 exid:k12phys-ch16-ex010 context-cnxmod:e0341ce1-48d3-4aaa-ab7c-096493a522a1 context-cnxmod:c47dd6d3-afa5-4546-b430-d0e3c24108d5 context-cnxmod:887a0746-dde7-4503-b376-fd07d8b68774 aplo:stax-apphys:6.E.4.2 dok:2 k12phys book:stax-phys book:stax-k12phys book:stax-apphys blooms:3 blooms:2 science-practice:argumentation filter-type:import:hs inbook-yes book-slug:college-physics-ap-courses module-slug:college-physics-ap-courses:25-2-the-law-of-reflection assignment-type:reading time:medium" data-is-vocab="false">
    <div data-type="exercise-question" data-is-answer-order-important="true" data-formats="free-response multiple-choice" id="243268"><span class="os-number">4</span>
<span class="os-divider">. </span>

<div class="os-problem-container">
  
  
  <div data-type="question-stem">
  <p>Use the law of reflection to explain why it is not a good idea to polish a mirror with coarse sandpaper.</p>
</div>
  <ol data-type="question-answers" type="a">
            <li data-type="question-answer" data-correctness="">
                <div data-type="answer-content">The surface becomes smooth. A smooth surface produces a sharp image.</div>
                
            </li>
            <li data-type="question-answer" data-correctness="">
                <div data-type="answer-content">The surface becomes irregular. An irregular surface produces a sharp image.</div>
                
            </li>
            <li data-type="question-answer" data-correctness="">
                <div data-type="answer-content">The surface becomes smooth. A smooth surface transmits but does not reflect light.</div>
                
            </li>
            <li data-type="question-answer" data-correctness="">
                <div data-type="answer-content">The surface becomes irregular. An irregular surface produces a blurred image.</div>
                
            </li>
            </ol>
</div>

        
        
        </div>
    </div><div data-type="injected-exercise" class="os-embed autogenerated-content" data-injected-from-nickname="" data-injected-from-version="7" data-injected-from-url="https://exercises.openstax.org/api/exercises?q=tag:k12phys-ch16-ot005" data-tags="os-practice-concepts k12phys-ch16 k12phys-ch16-s01 k12phys-ch16-s01-lo01 k12phys-ch16-ot005 context-cnxmod:e0341ce1-48d3-4aaa-ab7c-096493a522a1 context-cnxmod:c4cef576-059d-4369-9494-6484d1d6191c lo:stax-phys:25-7-2 context-cnxmod:ed675322-fbca-4fda-a451-3ba66c1c95cc aplo:stax-apphys:6.E.4.2 dok:1 k12phys book:stax-phys book:stax-k12phys time:short filter-type:import:hs tutor-only book-slug:college-physics-ap-courses module-slug:college-physics-ap-courses:25-7-image-formation-by-mirrors assignment-type:reading blooms:1" data-is-vocab="false">
    <div data-type="exercise-question" data-is-answer-order-important="true" data-formats="free-response multiple-choice" id="243266"><span class="os-number">5</span>
<span class="os-divider">. </span>

<div class="os-problem-container">
  
  
  <div data-type="question-stem">An object is placed in front of a concave mirror at a distance that is greater than the focal length of the mirror. Will the image produced by the mirror be real or virtual? Will it be erect or inverted?</div>
  <ol data-type="question-answers" type="a">
            <li data-type="question-answer" data-correctness="">
                <div data-type="answer-content">It is real and erect.</div>
                
            </li>
            <li data-type="question-answer" data-correctness="">
                <div data-type="answer-content">It is real and inverted.</div>
                
            </li>
            <li data-type="question-answer" data-correctness="">
                <div data-type="answer-content">It is virtual and inverted.</div>
                
            </li>
            <li data-type="question-answer" data-correctness="">
                <div data-type="answer-content">It is virtual and erect.</div>
                
            </li>
            </ol>
</div>

        
        
        </div>
    </div></section>






















